# Prime Hecke recurrences determine a coefficient sequence from its first term

Theorem statement: `MTT.coeff_eq_of_hecke_recurrence` (`1df17bb6-50c5-485a-898a-85f18e2dbda6`), by
cbirkbeck, 2026-09-06.

Proof: submission `2b058ec5-ecb6-445f-9a88-3bf11acd4dbe`, by cbirkbeck, 2026-09-06 (ACCEPTED);
locally adapted.

Fix $k\ge0$ and two functions $e,a:\mathbf N\to\mathbf C$ (only their values at primes matter; in
applications $e_p=\varepsilon(p)$ and $a_p$ is the $p$-th Hecke eigenvalue). Say that a sequence
$(b_m)_{m\ge0}$ of complex numbers satisfies the *prime Hecke recurrences* with data $(e,a)$ if for
every prime $p$ and every $m\ge0$
$$b_{pm}+e_p\,p^{k-1}\,[p\mid m]\,b_{m/p}=a_p\,b_m .$$
These are exactly the relations satisfied by the Fourier coefficients of a $T_p$-eigenform with
eigenvalue $a_p$ and nebentypus $\varepsilon$ (Diamond–Shurman, Proposition 5.8.5), by the
$q$-expansion formula for $T_p$.

**Claim.** If $(b_m)$ and $(c_m)$ both satisfy the prime Hecke recurrences with the same data
$(e,a)$, and $c_1=1$, then
$$b_m=b_1\,c_m\qquad\text{for all } m\ge1 .$$

*Proof.* Strong induction on $m$. For $m=1$ this is $c_1=1$. For $m\ge2$ choose a prime $p\mid m$
and write $m=pm'$ with $1\le m'<m$. The recurrence at $m'$ gives
$$b_{pm'}=a_p\,b_{m'}-e_p\,p^{k-1}[p\mid m']\,b_{m'/p},$$
and likewise for $c$; both $m'$ and $m'/p$ are smaller than $m$ and positive, so the inductive
hypothesis substitutes $b_{m'}=b_1c_{m'}$ and $b_{m'/p}=b_1c_{m'/p}$, giving $b_{pm'}=b_1c_{pm'}$.
$\square$

This is the uniqueness half of the statement that a normalised eigenform is determined by its Hecke
eigenvalues: applied with $c_m=a_m(f)$ for a normalised eigenform $f$ and $b_m=a_m(g)$ for a cusp
form $g$ with the same eigenvalues at every prime, it gives $a_m(g)=a_1(g)\,a_m(f)$ for $m\ge1$. In
Lean, $[p\mid m]\,b_{m/p}$ is `if p ∣ m then b (m / p) else 0` with natural-number division.

## Explanation of the source proof

We show that two sequences $(b_m)$, $(c_m)$ satisfying the same prime Hecke recurrences
$$b_{pm}+e_p\,p^{k-1}[p\mid m]\,b_{m/p}=a_p\,b_m\qquad(p\text{ prime},\ m\ge0)$$
with $c_1=1$ are proportional on $m\ge1$: $b_m=b_1c_m$.

The proof is a strong induction on $m$ (`Nat.strong_induction_on`). For $m=1$ the claim reads
$b_1=b_1c_1$, which is $c_1=1$. For $m\ge2$ pick a prime divisor $p$ of $m$
(`Nat.exists_prime_and_dvd`) and write $m=pm'$; then $m'\ge1$ and $m'<pm'$ because $p\ge2$
(`Nat.lt_mul_iff_one_lt_left`). Solving the recurrence at $m'$ for the top term,
$$b_{pm'}=a_p\,b_{m'}-e_p\,p^{k-1}[p\mid m']\,b_{m'/p},\qquad c_{pm'}=a_p\,c_{m'}-e_p\,p^{k-1}[p\mid
m']\,c_{m'/p}.$$
The inductive hypothesis applies to $m'$, and—when $p\mid m'$—to $m'/p$, which is positive
(`Nat.div_pos`) and smaller than $m$ (`Nat.div_le_self`); it gives $b_{m'}=b_1c_{m'}$ and $[p\mid
m']b_{m'/p}=b_1[p\mid m']c_{m'/p}$ (the latter by cases on $p\mid m'$). Substituting into the two
displayed identities, $b_{pm'}=b_1\big(a_pc_{m'}-e_p\,p^{k-1}[p\mid m']c_{m'/p}\big)=b_1c_{pm'}$,
and `ring` closes the goal.

This is the uniqueness argument behind Diamond–Shurman, Proposition 5.8.5: a sequence satisfying the
eigenform recurrences is determined by its first term. In the mission it is applied to $c_m=a_m(f)$
for the normalised eigenform $f$ and $b_m=a_m(g)$ for a cusp form $g$ with the same prime
eigenvalues.

